  "image_name": "3e134d422fb4d3072ab864b1cadab84e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how autophagy supports cellular protection by clearing material either broadly or in a targeted way. On the left, nonselective autophagy is triggered by nutrient deprivation: an isolation membrane forms an autophagosome, which fuses with a lysosome to degrade its contents and recycle nutrients, contributing to ATP production.\n\nThe center emphasizes selective autophagy, especially mitophagy and ER-phagy. Damaged mitochondria or parts of the endoplasmic reticulum are marked by cargo ubiquitylation, enclosed in autophagic vesicles, and delivered to lysosomes for breakdown. Mitophagy is linked to reducing reactive oxygen species and oxidative stress, while ER-phagy appears to remove glucose and lipid by-products and relieve ER stress.\n\nAcross the top, autophagic flux is shown as a regulated hub: mTOR inhibits it, whereas SIRT1, SIRT3, AMPK, and PGC1α promote it. These pathways feed into downstream benefits shown on the right and bottom of the diagram, including increased mitochondrial biogenesis, reduced inflammation and fibrosis, improved ATP production, and overall cytoprotection. Overall, the image presents autophagy as a quality-control system that maintains energy balance and limits cellular stress by degrading dysfunctional organelles and recycling their components.",
